    "q-333715": {
      "dom_id": "step-q333715",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"30px\" align=\"center\">${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"30px\" align=\"center\">${e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td width=\"30px\" align=\"center\">${e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr></tbody></table>",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-2t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}r(-2t)={e}^{-2t} \\\\ r(2t)={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-2t} \\\\ 2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtraction the second equation from the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-4t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-2t}-{e}^{2t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4t}({e}^{-2t}-{e}^{2t}).\n$$\n\n- Adding the first and the second equations, we get\n\n$$\n2{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-2t}+{e}^{2t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-2t}+{e}^{2t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-2t}+{e}^{2t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}) \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4t}({e}^{-4t}-{e}^{4t}))t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{2t}-{e}^{-2t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{2t}-{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}-{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
